      {
        "answer": "Light energy is converted into chemical energy by the green pigment molecule, chlorophyll, and involves two sets of reactions, the light reaction, and the dark reaction. The chloroplast contains columns of stacked discs called a granum, and each disc is membrane-bound structure called a thylakoid. Light penetrates through the plant cell and all these structures before it reaches the chlorophyll molecule. The light energy energizes a high energy electron on the chlorophyll molecule, which is released, and through a series of oxidation/reduction reactions, NADPH and ATP are generated. These intermediates of the light reaction then feed into the dark reaction of photosynthesis that ultimately creates organic molecules such as glucose through carbon fixation.",
